==Problem 22==
The integers from <imath> 1 </imath> through <imath> 25 </imath> are arbitrarily separated into five groups of <imath> 5 </imath> numbers each. The median of each group is identified. Let <imath> M </imath> equal the median of the five medians. What is the least possible value of <imath> M </imath>?

<imath>\textbf{(A) }9 \qquad \textbf{(B) }10 \qquad \textbf{(C) }12 \qquad \textbf{(D) }13 \qquad \textbf{(E) }14</imath>

==Solution 1==
<center>
<asy>
     unitsize(5mm);

     fill((0,2) -- (3,2) -- (3,5) -- (0,5) -- cycle, mediumgray+opacity(0.4));
     draw(box((0,0),(5,5)));
     draw((0,1) -- (5,1));
     draw((0,2) -- (5,2));
     draw((0,3) -- (5,3));
     draw((0,4) -- (5,4));
     draw((1,0) -- (1,5));
     draw((2,0) -- (2,5));
     draw((3,0) -- (3,5));
     draw((4,0) -- (4,5));

     draw((6,2.5) -- (7,2.5),arrow=ArcArrow(SimpleHead));

     fill((8,2) -- (11,2) -- (11,5) -- (8,5) -- cycle, mediumgray+opacity(0.4));
     draw(box((8,0),(13,5)));
     draw((8,1) -- (13,1));
     draw((8,2) -- (13,2));
     draw((8,3) -- (13,3));
     draw((8,4) -- (13,4));
     draw((9,0) -- (9,5));
     draw((10,0) -- (10,5));
     draw((11,0) -- (11,5));
     draw((12,0) -- (12,5));
     draw(box((10,2),(11,3)), black+2bp);

     label("$9$",(10.5,2.5));
</asy>
</center>

Consider using a five by five square table to solve this problem. The rows represent the five groups, the center column is the median of each group and the center square cell is the least possible value of \(M\). The only numbers that really matter is the ones in the shaded area, the rest can be ignored. In order to find the least possible value of \(M\) the smallest numbers, \(1 ... 9\), need to be in these nine square cells. \(9\) is the smallest number that is able to satisfy both median of a group and median of five medians. Final answer, the least possible value of \(M\) is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A)}~9}.</imath> ~TutorJack

==Solution 2 (Quick and easy)==
If a group has median <imath>m</imath>, then we must have that <imath>3</imath> of the numbers in that group are <imath>\leq m</imath>. Since there are 5 different groups, <imath>3</imath> groups must have a median <imath>\leq M</imath>, so there are at least <imath>3\cdot3=9</imath> numbers that are <imath>\leq M</imath>. Since there are at least <imath>9</imath> numbers that are <imath>\leq M</imath>, we have <imath>M</imath> at minimum <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A) }9}.</imath> ~Baihly2024

==Solution 3 (Solution 1 Rigorously)==

Let the groupings be <imath> G_1, G_2, \dots, G_5 </imath>. Let the medians of each of the groupings be <imath> m_1, m_2, m_3, m_4, </imath> and <imath> m_5 </imath> in that order. Let <imath> M </imath> be the median of those medians. Then, we wish to minimize <imath> M </imath>. This can be possible if we have three elements in <imath> G_1 </imath> less than <imath> M </imath>, three elements in <imath> G_2 </imath> less than <imath> M </imath>, and three elements in <imath> G_3 </imath> less than an equal to <imath> M </imath> are there (as the last number itself is <imath> M </imath>). Thus, the smallest way to choose <imath> 9 </imath> such numbers is <imath> \{1, 2, 3, 4, \dots, 9\} </imath>, in which <imath> M </imath> is the largest of these, and thus the answer is <imath> \boxed{9} </imath>.

~Pinotation


==Solution 4==
If you want to prove a solution one of the easiest ways is just to list an example of it.
A median has to be greater than the first two numbers of the group.
Then we just list the groups out.
This includes:

\begin{align*}
( 1,2,3,16,17) ,\ ( 4,5,6,18,19) ,\ ( 7,8,9,20,21) ,\ ( 10,11,12,22,23) ,\ ( 13,14,15,24,25) & & 
\end{align*}

Then according to this triple we are able to find the <imath> M </imath> is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A) }9} </imath>.

~Lynn403

